\mt1 Editor’s Introduction
\ip This volume brings together two English translations that have long been read apart. The Old Testament is Sir Lancelot Brenton’s English translation of the Septuagint, as printed in 1870. The New Testament is the King James Version in the text prepared by F. H. A. Scrivener for the Cambridge Paragraph Bible of 1873, conformed to the Byzantine text of the Greek New Testament as edited by Maurice A. Robinson and William G. Pierpont (2026) wherever that text differs from the Received Text in a way that English can show. Both English texts come from eBible.org, and Brenton’s includes the corrections eBible has made to it.
\ip The Septuagint is the Old Testament of the Orthodox Church, and it is, as Brenton says in his preface, “the Old Testament translated into the language of the New.” Most English Bibles translate the Old Testament from the Hebrew, so a reader seeking the Greek must look elsewhere. Brenton wrote in the style of English of the King James Bible, which has also been used to translate the services of the Church. The two testaments thus speak with one voice. The name Orthodox Liturgical English Bible reflects these choices: Orthodox for its Old Testament and the order of its books, which follows the Orthodox Church; liturgical for its English.
\ip Each testament opens with its translation’s prefaces and introductions. Brenton’s preface and introduction come before Genesis. The Epistle Dedicatory and The Translators to the Reader stand before Matthew. Scrivener’s critical introduction and appendices were never part of the online text and are not printed here. Brenton’s list of abbreviations follows the table of contents, supplemented with others used in the notes. The notes of his Appendix are printed as footnotes at the verses they discuss, and the passages it supplies from the Alexandrine text stand in their books. The opinions expressed in these pages are those of their original authors.
\ip The books that English Bibles set apart as the Apocrypha are printed in their Greek places, so there is no separate Apocrypha section: the Prayer of Manasses follows 2 Chronicles, 4 Maccabees follows 3 Maccabees, and the additions to Esther and Daniel and Psalm 151 stand within the text. Ezra and Nehemiah are printed as two books, 2 Esdras and Nehemias, and the chapters and verses are numbered as in the Greek. Most books keep their Septuagint names. Others are renamed to follow the same practice: Brenton’s Joshua is Jesus, the Son of Navi, and his four books of Kings are the four books of Kingdoms. Hosea and Nahum take their familiar English names in place of his Osee and Naum.
\ip The translations are preserved with a few exceptions. Brenton translated Valpy’s printed Greek edition, derived from the Sixtine or Roman edition of 1587, and gave readings from Codex Alexandrinus in his notes and Appendix. The Sixtine edition was based chiefly on Codex Vaticanus, but supplied its gaps and altered some of its readings from other manuscripts. Where Swete’s edition supports the wording and placement of a reading, it is printed in the text. The displaced English appears in a footnote marked “Brenton”, or with the name of a manuscript where Swete establishes that reading. “Vat.” names Codex Vaticanus; correcting hands and doubtful readings are qualified. Where the Hebrew agrees with the printed reading, the note says so. Where Brenton gives such a reading only in Greek, or his English for it is wrong or incomplete, the words his English lacks are taken from R. R. Ottley’s translation of Esaias from Codex Alexandrinus (1909) or, where the Hebrew reads with Alexandrinus, from the King James Version or its revision of 1885; wording from Ottley or the revision is adapted into King James English, and each supplied word is declared with its source. Doubtful readings remain where he left them. The missing verse at {1SA 23:12} and the missing verses at {1KI 14:1-20} are supplied from the King James Version, the latter conformed to Alexandrinus where noted; at {1KI 7:38-50} the verses stand before verse 1, where Alexandrinus has them, with a note at each place. In the New Testament, the King James translators followed the Received Text, the Greek of the printed editions of their day; the Orthodox Church reads the Byzantine text, which the great majority of the Greek manuscripts carry. Where the two differ and the difference can be seen in English, the King James words are changed, as little as the Greek requires and in the translators’ own English, and a note gives the words they had, marked TR where the apparatuses of Robinson and Pierpont and of Boyd show Scrivener’s reconstruction of the Received Text and Stephanus’s edition agreeing, and otherwise marked KJV, as the Old Testament’s are marked Brenton; where the difference cannot be seen in English, nothing is changed. Four verses of the Received Text that the Byzantine manuscripts lack, one in Luke and three in Acts, are omitted, each quoted in a note on the verse before it; the doxology of Romans stands at {ROM 14:24-26}, where those manuscripts have it, with a note at each place; and {MAT 23:13} and {MAT 23:14} exchange places. The appendix of readings at the end of the volume lists every verse in which the two Greek texts differ, with the Greek of both and, where the English changes, the words of both Bibles. The changes start from William G. Pierpont’s Some Improvements to the King James Version from the Majority Greek Manuscripts (1990). Where an instruction cannot be applied or its wording does not fit, a fitting parallel in the King James Version is preferred, then Pierpont’s wording, the Revised Version of 1881, and Robert Adam Boyd’s American Standard Version, Byzantine Text (2021). Boyd’s Text-Critical English New Testament (2022) and Graham G. Thomason’s Far Above All are occasionally consulted, in that order. Borrowed wording is adapted into the English of the King James Version; the appendix names the other witnesses consulted. Shared-Greek corrections of the King James English preserve the former rendering in an “or,” note, reusing the 1611 margin where it already gives that comparison. At {PSA 67:16}, Brenton’s “curdled like cheese” gloss is printed in the text, with his former “swelling mountain” in the margin; both render the same Greek.
\ip Brenton’s footnotes are printed, with a few slips corrected, those the online text lacks supplied from the transcription of the Christian Classics Ethereal Library. In the notes and the front and back matter of both translations, citations use this edition’s book names and verse numbers. Abbreviations and numbers follow The Chicago Manual of Style; the wording is otherwise unchanged. The online text of the New Testament has no notes, so it carries instead the marginal notes of the King James Bible of 1611, as transcribed by Calvin George, without their cross-references, besides the notes marked TR or KJV; a 1611 note whose reading the Byzantine text prints, or which glosses words of the Received Text the edition no longer has, is left out. Words that the translators supplied to complete the English sense, which many Bibles print in italics, are set here in ordinary type. The title pages, the table of contents, and the headings are this edition’s own.
\ip Where the New Testament quotes the Old, a link at the first verse of each passage names the full passage at the other end. The link also records Turpie’s judgment (1868) on the quotation’s wording: “Heb. + LXX” where it follows the Hebrew and the Septuagint alike, allowing differences in wording. In “Heb. ≠ LXX” and “LXX ≠ Heb.”, it follows the first text named and differs from the second. This meaning applies at both ends of the link. A link without a gloss marks a quotation that he judged to follow neither, or to depart from both by whole clauses, as when a verse is abridged or two passages are joined. Turpie compares the Greek and Hebrew texts, rather than the two English translations.
\ip Links use this edition’s chapter and verse numbers, which are the Greek’s and sometimes differ from the King James Bible’s. The psalms are numbered as in the Greek, most of them one lower, and the title of a psalm counts as its first verse or two, so that {PSA 33:13-17} is the King James Bible’s {kjv bare PSA 33:13-17}. From chapter 25 on, the chapters of Jeremias follow a different order, so that {JER 38:31-34} is the King James Bible’s {kjv bare JER 38:31-34}. Where the Septuagint adds to a verse, the addition may be set apart as a lettered verse, which the King James Bible lacks: the cheerful giver of {2CO 9:7} is found at {PRO 22:8a}. The table that follows this introduction gives the King James Bible’s number wherever it differs, and takes the place of Brenton’s table of the chapters of Jeremias.
\ip The page size is ISO B5 (176 × 250 mm). Scripture is set in one column in Utopia at 9 pt, with the notes in the inside margin beside their verses, and at the foot of the page those that would crowd it. The design is based on the Berean Standard Bible layout that comes with PTXprint, a free program for typesetting Bibles.
